n  figures are released from ballots  counted where the polls closed an hour  earlier  bernard smith al jazeera paris  all right natasha butler joins us live  from paris natasha those early turnout  figures weren't looking so good is it  picking up now  well at this polling station in central  paris has certainly been quite a lot of  people coming to vote there was quite a  queue behind me a short while ago but it  seems that perhaps that is not the  picture elsewhere in the country because  we have had uh the turnout figures at  midday uh french time and according to  the french interior ministry 25  of eligible voters have actually cast  their ballot  it's low but it's not drastically low  it's three points uh lower than it was  in 2017 there were 28 percent uh had  cast their votes at around the same time  but you know there has been little  enthusiasm for this election it really  seems as if the campaign's never really  uh got off the ground you might say  that's at least what many people have  been saying in uh recent weeks and  people are feeling quite weary we've had  the covert pandemic for a couple of  years people are very preoccupied  looking at the war in ukraine and there  is this sense that we saw in 2017  amongst people that they're simply fed  up with the political system they're fed  up with politicians they're fed up of uh  politics and they don't feel massively  motivated nevertheless they certainly  have a certain amount of choice you've  got 12 presidential candidates  everything from anti-capitalists to the  far right and everything in between  including uh the french president  emmanuel macron who wants a second term  his main challenger marine le pen of the  far right has certainly run a very tight  campaign according to her supporters  then we might have jeanette malon  from the far left also posing something  of a challenge perhaps even making it uh  to the second round it is interesting  though i was speaking to one woman a bit  earlier who said to me that she hankered  for the old days she said uh back then  you had two main political parties in  france you had the conservatives on the  right you had the socialists on the left  and she said it actually made it so much  easier to pick  and natasha let's take us through  the some of the key issues which are  facing voters right now  well the main concern really for most  voters in france has been  the cost of living it is something that  people will talk about repeatedly  wherever you are in the country they are  seeing prices rise the the cost of food  uh the cost of petrol people are very  worried indeed about uh about their bank  accounts about their money at the end of  the month they're struggling financially  it was an issue during the yellow vest  protest a few years ago it is still an  issue today and that is also linked to  another big concern  in this election that of course is  russia's invasion of ukraine people  looking at that very carefully indeed  they see a war in europe they see europe  in crisis some of them are frightened  they're worried and they see the impact  also  on prices  other issues immigration of course that  has played very heavily in this campaign  particularly uh with uh the far-right  parties and the uh emergence last year  of a new uh face in this race if you  like eric zamor a former tv pundit who  is also taken a very tough and hard line  on immigration and then you have other  issues things like climate change they  haven't actually been uh perhaps at the  forefront of many people's minds during  this election perhaps they haven't been  a top of a gender topic for some of the  candidates but still of course one of  the main uh one of the main issues for  some  all right thanks so much and fascia  butler there  well emmanuel macron is a former banker  and economic adviser to president  francois hollande  his centrist party aims to draw support  from the mainstream left and right as  president he introduced unpopular  pension reforms and faced the country's  long-running yellow vest protests  marine le pen is running for president  for the third time she succeeded her  father as leader of the far-right  national front party rebranding it as  the national rally  staying in paris victor male is the  paris bureau chief for the financial  times and joins us good to have you with  us  so is it the cost of living that is the  deciding question for most voters  i think i think  that's true i mean your correspondence  mentioned it and it is the issue that  has really come up in the last few weeks  of the campaign and it is the thing that  people are most concerned about and it's  also the  uh the theme that marine le pen has  actually gone on about quite a lot very  successfully on her sort of trips around  the country to the villages and  towns across the country and she's  emphasized that rather than emphasizing  the policies for which she's better  known which are essentially  anti-immigration policies and uh  hostility towards nato and the eu so  this issue of prices cost of living uh  although it's actually less severe in  france than it is in other european  countries it's still very much the kind  of theme of the campaign let me jump in  then let me jump in and ask you this  question because it was really  interesting that you mentioned there in  the last few weeks i'm wondering is  there a connection with the war in  ukraine  in terms of both the rising cost of of  living in france the cost of petrol and  and so on and also the pivot in the in  the rhetoric of of candidates like  marine le pen as you mentioned they're  pivoting away from  attacking nato and eu and towards other  issues  yeah i mean i think you're right the the  certainly the war sort of very  steeply pushed up the cost of petrol and  and diesel uh and gas although actually  the french consumer is not with vehicle  fuel but with home heating and so on  they're actually quite protected  from those price rises o