e  pen who is probably likely to be the  person with him in the second round all  right let's broaden it out for our  global audience and and talk about this  election in terms bigger than france  what's at stake here is it  is this election basically going to  determine whether european populism  anti-eu  anti-multilateralism is ascendant or  descended is is that what's also at  stake here  well yes but i would say that even if uh  york city right but even if marine le  pen does not win this one she is almost  certain to have the highest score that  the far right has ever had in a  presidential election in france which  means that the threat will be greater  than it's ever been  even if she doesn't actually become  president on april 24th so  whichever way you look at it  it is very much still in the ascendant i  think the whole the whole sort of  movement towards nationalism towards  populism if you like uh certainly  towards sort of hostility towards  globalism or globalists like uh like  emmanuel macron you know we saw it with  the election of donald trump uh in 2016  we saw it before that in the same year  with the brexit vote  and we've seen it in many other  countries around the world where sort of  uh you know anti-internationalist very  nationalist very populist leaders tend  to tend to win power  all right it's good to get your thoughts  thanks so much for joining us victor  thank you

